## Options API: dot-delimited path

The `watch` option key can be a simple dot-delimited path [5]:

```js
export default {
  watch: {
    // Note: only simple paths. Expressions are not supported.
    'some.nested.key'(newValue) {
      // ...
    }
  }
}
```

Complex expressions are **not** supported — only dot-delimited paths; for complex sources use the imperative `$watch()` API instead [5].

## Deep watchers (fire on any nested mutation)

`watch` is shallow by default and won't trigger on nested property changes, so use `deep: true` to fire on all nested mutations [1]:

```js
export default {
  watch: {
    someObject: {
      handler(newValue, oldValue) {},
      deep: true
    }
  }
}
```

## Composition API

Watching a getter fires only when the returned object is replaced; add `deep: true` to catch nested mutations [1]:

```js
watch(
  () => state.someObject,
  (newValue, oldValue) => {},
  { deep: true }
)
```

Calling `watch()` directly on a reactive object implicitly creates a deep watcher [1]. In Vue 3.5+, `deep` can be a number setting the max traversal depth [1].

Note: deep watching traverses all nested properties and can be expensive on large data structures [1]. If you only need a few nested properties, `watchEffect()` may be more efficient since it tracks only the properties used in the callback [4].

## Props (the usual way)

Data normally flows from parent to child via props, which form a **one-way-down binding**: when the parent property updates it flows down to the child, but not the other way around [3]. Every time the parent updates, all props in the child are refreshed with the latest value, so you should **not** mutate a prop inside the child — Vue will warn you [3].

```js
const props = defineProps(['foo'])

// ❌ warning, props are readonly!
props.foo = 'bar'
```

If the child needs to own the value, copy it into local state or a computed [3]:

```js
const props = defineProps(['initialCounter'])

// counter only uses props.initialCounter as the initial value;
// it is disconnected from future prop updates.
const counter = ref(props.initialCounter)
```

Note that object/array props can still have their nested properties mutated by the child, since they're passed by reference — avoid this unless parent and child are tightly coupled; usually the child should emit an event and let the parent mutate [5].

## Deeply nested children: provide / inject

Passing the same prop down a long parent chain ("props drilling") is painful [2]. Instead, a parent can act as a **dependency provider** with `provide`, and any descendant, however deep, can `inject` it [2].

## Slots (render functions)

You can also pass content down as slot functions, including scoped slots that receive data back from the child [1][4]:

```js
h(MyComponent, null, {
  default: () => 'default slot',
  foo: () => h('div', 'foo')
})
```

The `null` is required so the slots object isn't treated as props [4].

A computed property declaratively derives a value from other reactive state, and Vue automatically tracks its reactive dependencies [4].

**Options API** — declare them under the `computed` option, where each key is the property name and the value is a getter function [3]:

```js
export default {
  data() {
    return { a: 1 }
  },
  computed: {
    aDouble() {
      return this.a * 2
    }
  }
}
```
Getters have `this` bound to the component instance; with an arrow function, use the first argument (`vm`) instead [3].

**Composition API** — use `computed()` with a getter function [5]:

```js
const numbers = ref([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])
const evenNumbers = computed(() => numbers.value.filter((n) => n % 2 === 0))
```

A few notes:
- Computed properties are cached based on their reactive dependencies and only re-evaluate when those change, unlike methods which run on every re-render [4].
- They're getter-only by default; pass an object with `get` and `set` to make one writable [1].
- Getters should be pure — no mutating other state, async requests, or DOM changes — and the returned value should be treated as read-only [2].

A custom directive is an object of lifecycle hooks (similar to a component's) that receive the element the directive is bound to [1].

**In `<script setup>`** — any camelCase variable starting with the `v` prefix is usable as a directive, so `vHighlight` becomes `v-highlight` [1]:

```vue
<script setup>
const vHighlight = {
  mounted: (el) => {
    el.classList.add('is-highlight')
  }
}
</script>

<template>
  <p v-highlight>This sentence is important!</p>
</template>
```

**Without `<script setup>`** — register locally via the `directives` option [1]:

```js
export default {
  directives: {
    highlight: { /* ... */ }
  }
}
```

**Globally** — use `app.directive()`, passing a name and a definition [1][5]:

```js
app.directive('highlight', { /* ... */ })
```

Other notes:
- If `mounted` and `updated` share the same behavior, you can pass a function shorthand instead of an object [4]:

```js
app.directive('color', (el, binding) => {
  el.style.color = binding.value
})
```
- Directives must be registered before use in templates, just like components [1].
- Only use custom directives when the functionality requires direct DOM manipulation; prefer built-in declarative directives like `v-bind` where possible [2].
- Applying directives to components isn't recommended, since they apply to the root node and are ignored (with a warning) on multi-root components [4].

## With `<script setup>` (recommended)

Use pure type annotations on `defineProps()` [4]:

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
defineProps<{
  title?: string
  likes?: number
}>()
</script>
```

Complex types work fine with type-based declaration [1]:

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
interface Book {
  title: string
  author: string
  year: number
}

const props = defineProps<{
  book: Book
}>()
</script>
```

## Without `<script setup>` / Options API

Wrap the component in `defineComponent()` so props types are inferred from the `props` option — including `required: true` and `default` [1][2][5]:

```ts
import { defineComponent } from 'vue'

export default defineComponent({
  props: {
    name: String,
    msg: { type: String, required: true }
  },
  setup(props) {
    props.msg // type: string
  }
})
```

## Runtime declaration with complex types

Runtime `props` options only accept constructor functions, so use the `PropType` utility type for objects with nested properties or function signatures [2]:

```ts
import { defineComponent } from 'vue'
import type { PropType } from 'vue'

export default defineComponent({
  props: {
    book: { type: Object as PropType<Book>, required: true },
    callback: Function as PropType<(id: number) => void>
  }
})
```

This also works with `defineProps({ book: Object as PropType<Book> })` [1].

## Caveat

On TypeScript below 4.7, use arrow functions for `default` and `validator` prop options, otherwise inference of `this` inside them can fail [3].

Use the app instance's `.component()` method, which makes the component available in the template of any component in that app [1].

```js
import { createApp } from 'vue'

const app = createApp({})

app.component(
  // the registered name
  'MyComponent',
  // the implementation
  {
    /* ... */
  }
)
```

- With SFCs, pass the imported `.vue` file as the implementation [1]:

```js
import MyComponent from './App.vue'

app.component('MyComponent', MyComponent)
```

- Calls can be chained: `app.component('ComponentA', ComponentA).component('ComponentB', ComponentB)` [1].
- Globally registered components are also available inside each other and in all subcomponents [1].

Trade-offs: global registration prevents tree-shaking of unused components and makes dependency relationships less explicit in large apps, so local registration is often preferred [2].

`shallowRef()` is the shallow version of `ref()` [1].

- The inner value is stored and exposed as-is and is **not** made deeply reactive — only `.value` access is reactive [1].
- It's typically used for performance optimization of large data structures, or for integrating with external state management systems [1].
- Trade-off: nested objects must be treated as immutable, and updates are triggered by replacing the root value [3].
- You can force effects to re-run after deep mutations with `triggerRef()` [2].

```js
const state = shallowRef({ count: 1 })

// does NOT trigger change
state.value.count = 2

// does trigger change
state.value = { count: 2 }
```
[1]

Use `toRefs` when you have a **reactive object whose properties you want to destructure or spread without losing reactivity** [2]. It converts a reactive object into a plain object where each property is a ref linked to the source property (each created via `toRef()`) [2].

Typical cases:

- **Returning state from a composable**, so consumers can destructure it [2]:

```js
function useFeatureX() {
  const state = reactive({ foo: 1, bar: 2 })
  // convert to refs when returning
  return toRefs(state)
}

const { foo, bar } = useFeatureX()
```

- **Destructuring props in `setup()`**, since plain destructuring loses reactivity [3]:

```js
const { title } = toRefs(props) // `title` tracks `props.title`
```

When to prefer `toRef` instead:

- For a **single property** rather than the whole object [1][3].
- For a property that **may not exist yet** — `toRefs` only generates refs for properties enumerable at call time, while `toRef` returns a usable ref for optional properties [2][1].

## Using `setup()` in an Options API component

The `setup()` hook is the entry point for Composition API, and one of its main use cases is integrating Composition-API-based code into an Options API component [1]. This is supported — you can use both APIs in the same component — though it's mainly recommended when an existing Options API codebase needs to integrate new features or external libraries written with Composition API [3].

Declare reactive state with the Reactivity APIs and return an object; its properties become available both in the template and on the component instance, so other options can access them via `this` [1]:

```vue
<script>
import { ref } from 'vue'

export default {
  setup() {
    const count = ref(0)

    // expose to template and other options API hooks
    return {
      count
    }
  },

  mounted() {
    console.log(this.count) // 0
  }
}
</script>

<template>
  <button @click="count++">{{ count }}</button>
</template>
```

Things to keep in mind:

- refs are shallow-unwrapped in the template and on `this`, so no `.value` is needed there [1].
- `setup()` has no access to the instance — `this` is `undefined` inside it; Options API can read Composition-API-exposed values, but not the reverse [1].
- It must return an object *synchronously*; `async setup()` is only allowed when the component is a descendant of a `Suspense` component [1].
- If you're using Single-File Components, `<script setup>` is strongly recommended instead for a more succinct syntax [1].

## Computed properties

Use a computed property instead of putting complex reactive logic in templates [4]. A computed property automatically tracks its reactive dependencies and updates any bindings that depend on it when those dependencies change [3].

**Options API** — declare under `computed`; `this` is bound to the component instance [5]:

```js
export default {
  data() {
    return { a: 1 }
  },
  computed: {
    aDouble() {
      return this.a * 2
    }
  }
}
```
[5]

**Composition API** — `computed()` takes a getter and returns a *computed ref*; access it via `.value` in JS, auto-unwrapped in templates [4]:

```vue
<script setup>
import { reactive, computed } from 'vue'
const author = reactive({ books: ['Vue 3 - Basic Guide'] })
const publishedBooksMessage = computed(() => {
  return author.books.length > 0 ? 'Yes' : 'No'
})
</script>
<template><span>{{ publishedBooksMessage }}</span></template>
```
[4]

**Notes**
- Computed properties are cached based on their reactive dependencies, unlike methods, which re-run on every re-render; use a method if you don't want caching [3].
- They are getter-only by default; for a writable one, pass both `get` and `set` [1][5].
- Getters must be side-effect free, and the returned value should be treated as read-only [2].

